[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Instinct or Cleverness?]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.31]Lesson 54 | 第54课
[00:02.26]Instinct or cleverness? | 是本能还是机智
[00:10.16]Was the writer successful in protecting his peach tree? Why not? | 作者成功地保护了他的桃树吗？为什么没有？
[00:17.67]We have been brought up to fear insects. | 我们从小就被教育要害怕昆虫。
[00:20.65]We regard them as unnecessary creatures that do more harm than good. | 我们把它们视为害多益少的无用生物。
[00:26.54]We continually wage war on them, for they contaminate our food, carry diseases, or devour our crops. | 我们不断同它们开战，因为它们污染我们的食物、传播疾病，或吞食我们的庄稼。
[00:35.76]They sting or bite without provocation; they fly uninvited into our rooms on summer nights, or beat against our lighted windows. | 它们无缘无故地蜇人咬人；夏夜里它们不请自来飞进我们的房间，或扑打我们亮着灯的窗户。
[00:46.15]We live in dread not only of unpleasant insects like spiders or wasps, but of quite harmless ones like moths. | 我们不仅惧怕蜘蛛、黄蜂之类令人讨厌的昆虫，而且惧怕飞蛾之类完全无害的昆虫。
[00:55.33]Reading about them increases our understanding without dispelling our fears. | 阅读有关昆虫的书籍增进了我们的了解，却未能驱除我们的恐惧。
[01:01.67]Knowing that the industrious ant lives in a highly organized society | 知道勤劳的蚂蚁生活在组织高度严密的社会里
[01:07.57]does nothing to prevent us from being filled with revulsion when we find hordes of them crawling over a carefully prepared picnic lunch. | 也无济于事——当我们发现大群蚂蚁爬上我们精心准备的野餐时，心中仍充满厌恶。
[01:18.08]No matter how much we like honey, | 无论我们多么喜欢蜂蜜，
[01:20.89]or how much we have read about the uncanny sense of direction which bees possess, we have a horror of being stung. | 或者读过多少关于蜜蜂所拥有的不可思议的方向感的文章，我们都怕被蜇。
[01:30.32]Most of our fears are unreasonable, but they are impossible to erase. | 我们大多数的恐惧都是毫无道理的，却又不可能抹去。
[01:36.15]At the same time, however, insects are strangely fascinating. | 然而与此同时，昆虫又奇怪地令人着迷。
[01:41.48]We enjoy reading about them, especially when we find that, like the praying mantis, they lead perfectly horrible lives. | 我们喜欢阅读有关它们的书籍，尤其当我们发现，像螳螂那样，它们过着完全可怕的生活时。
[01:51.10]We enjoy staring at them, entranced as they go about their business, unaware (we hope) of our presence. | 我们喜欢盯着它们看，它们专注于自己的事务、（但愿）没有察觉我们的存在时，我们看得入了迷。
[01:59.91]Who has not stood in awe at the sight of a spider pouncing on a fly, | 谁没有敬畏地看过蜘蛛猛扑向苍蝇，
[02:04.96]or a column of ants triumphantly bearing home an enormous dead beetle? | 或一队蚂蚁得意洋洋地扛着一只巨大的死甲虫回家的场面呢？
[02:11.43]Last summer I spent days in the garden watching thousands of ants crawling up the trunk of my prize peach tree. | 去年夏天，我花了几天时间在花园里观察成千上万只蚂蚁爬上我那棵珍贵的桃树的树干。
[02:20.64]The tree has grown against a warm wall on a sheltered side of the house. | 这棵树是靠着房屋背风一面的暖墙生长的。
[02:26.08]I am especially proud of it, | 我特别为它感到自豪，
[02:28.61]not only because it has survived several severe winters, but because it occasionally produces luscious peaches. | 不仅因为它熬过了几个严冬，还因为它偶尔结出甘美的桃子。
[02:39.08]During the summer, I noticed that the leaves of the tree were beginning to wither. | 夏天里，我注意到树叶开始枯萎。
[02:45.01]Clusters of tiny insects called aphides were to be found on the underside of the leaves. | 在叶子背面发现了一簇簇名叫蚜虫的小昆虫。
[02:52.48]They were visited by a large colony of ants which obtained a sort of honey from them. | 一大群蚂蚁造访了它们，从它们身上获取某种蜜汁。
[02:59.12]I immediately embarked on an experiment which even though it failed to get rid of the ants kept me fascinated for twenty-four hours. | 我立即着手一项实验，这项实验虽然未能除掉蚂蚁，却让我着迷了24个小时。
[03:10.00]I bound the base of the tree with sticky tape, making it impossible for the ants to reach the aphides. | 我用粘胶带缠住树干底部，使蚂蚁无法爬到蚜虫那里。
[03:17.71]The tape was so sticky that they did not dare to cross it. | 胶带太粘了，它们不敢爬过去。
[03:22.59]For a long time, I watched them scurrying around the base of the tree in bewilderment. | 很长一段时间里，我看着它们困惑地在树干底部团团乱转。
[03:29.59]I even went out at midnight with a torch and noted with satisfaction (and surprise) | 我甚至在半夜拿着手电筒出去，满意地（也惊讶地）发现
[03:36.88]that the ants were still swarming around the sticky tape without being able to do anything about it. | 蚂蚁仍然蜂拥在粘胶带周围，却无计可施。
[03:43.94]I got up early next morning hoping to find that the ants had given up in despair. | 第二天清晨我早早起床，希望发现蚂蚁已经绝望地放弃了。
[03:49.84]Instead, I saw that they had discovered a new route. | 相反，我看到它们找到了一条新路线。
[03:54.31]They were climbing up the wall of the house and then on to the leaves of the tree. | 它们正沿着房子的墙往上爬，然后爬上树叶。
[03:59.58]I realized sadly that I had been completely defeated by their ingenuity. | 我悲哀地意识到，我已被它们的机智彻底击败了。
[04:05.79]The ants had been quick to find an answer to my thoroughly unscientific methods! | 蚂蚁很快就找到了对付我这完全不科学的方法的答案！
